# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  apps/api — backend environment
#
#  Copy to `.env` for local development. On the VPS (Phase 11) these go in the
#  PM2 ecosystem file or a `.env.production` that is never committed.
#
#  Anything a non-developer should be able to change at runtime (business name,
#  UPI ID, receipt footer) is NOT here — it lives in the `AppSetting` table so
#  the admin can edit it without a redeploy (context.txt §24).
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Database (Prisma / MySQL) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# mysql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE
# Read by prisma.config.ts and by lib/db.ts. Add ?connection_limit=5 if the VPS
# hits MySQL's max_connections.
DATABASE_URL="mysql://root:@localhost:3306/kdmc-parking"

# ── Auth.js / NextAuth v5 (Phase 02, decisions.md D3) ───────────────────────
# REQUIRED. Derives the encryption key for every session JWT — the API refuses to
# start without it. At least 32 characters:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Rotating this invalidates every mobile session — users must sign in again.
AUTH_SECRET=""

# Public origin of this API. Locally http://localhost:3000; in production the
# HTTPS domain behind Nginx (Phase 11).
#
# Its SCHEME matters beyond URL building: http vs https decides the session cookie
# name, which is also the JWT's HKDF salt (apps/api/lib/auth/session.ts). So
# switching this from http to https invalidates existing sessions, exactly as
# rotating AUTH_SECRET would — expect one round of re-logins on the day the VPS
# gets its certificate.
AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# How long a mobile session JWT stays valid, in days. Long-lived on purpose: a
# parking customer should not be logged out between visits.
AUTH_SESSION_MAX_AGE_DAYS="30"

# ── Google Sign-In (Phase 02, decisions.md D3) ──────────────────────────────
# The RN app obtains a Google ID token itself and posts it here; the backend
# verifies it with google-auth-library. Both client IDs below are valid
# *audiences* for that token, so both must be listed.
#
# From Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials:
#   - "Web application" client — also the one @react-native-google-signin needs
#     as `webClientId` to issue an ID token.
#   - "Android" client — package name + release/debug SHA-1 of the EAS build.
#
# Optional: with neither set, email/password sign-in works normally and
# POST /api/auth/google answers 503 with an explanation rather than the API
# refusing to boot.
GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID=""

# Only needed for the Phase 2 *website's* browser OAuth redirect flow. The
# native ID-token flow does not use it — leave empty until then.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# ── Booking rules (context.txt §15) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Minutes an unpaid/unapproved booking is held before the sweep expires it.
# Overridden at runtime by the `booking.expiryMinutes` AppSetting if present.
BOOKING_EXPIRY_MINUTES="10"

# Shared secret for the expiry-sweep endpoint (Phase 05). The system cron job on
# the VPS sends it as `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>`; without this the
# sweep URL would be world-callable.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
CRON_SECRET=""

# ── Expo push notifications (Phase 09, decisions.md D4) ─────────────────────
# Optional for development. Required in production once "Enhanced Security for
# Push Notifications" is enabled on the Expo project — create it with
# `npx eas-cli@latest token:create` (an Expo access token), not an API key.
EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=""

# ── File storage (Phase 15) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the UTR payment screenshot (and any future upload) is stored. One
# toggle, read by lib/upload/index.ts:
#   local     — written to apps/api/public/uploads on this server, served from
#               this API's own origin (AUTH_URL above). Needs no other config;
#               works out of the box on a fresh VPS. Remember to back up or
#               persist that directory across deploys (see public/README.md).
#   mediahost — posted to the self-hosted PHP service on
#               media.kumarinfotech.com. Needs MEDIAHOST_PROJECT_NAME/TOKEN
#               below.
STORAGE_PROVIDER="mediahost"

# Optional at boot — the UTR screenshot is optional by design (§278-336), so
# POST /api/bookings/:id/utr degrades to "no screenshot stored" rather than
# refusing to boot, with a clear runtime error only when a screenshot is
# actually posted and these are missing. Only read when STORAGE_PROVIDER=mediahost.
# Credentials for the project on media.kumarinfotech.com; never exposed to the
# RN client, only used server-side.
MEDIAHOST_PROJECT_NAME=""
MEDIAHOST_TOKEN=""

# ── Transactional email / Brevo (Phase 15 — forgot-password OTP) ────────────
# Used by exactly one flow: the 6-digit password-reset code emailed by
# POST /api/auth/forgot-password/request-otp.
#
# Optional at boot, like GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID above — the API has to keep taking
# bookings on a server where email was never set up. sendEmail() throws a
# readable error naming the missing variable only when a send is actually
# attempted (lib/notifications/email.ts).
#
# Brevo's HTTP API, not SMTP: a VPS provider blocking outbound 587 is the
# normal case, and a blocked SMTP send looks identical to a slow one.
#
# From Brevo dashboard → SMTP & API → API Keys. Starts "xkeysib-".
BREVO_API_KEY=""

# MUST be an address verified under Brevo → Senders. Brevo rejects a send from
# anything else, so an unverified from-address fails at the API call — the code
# is stored, no email arrives, and only the server log says why.
EMAIL_FROM="bhavesh.gharat.kit@gmail.com"

# Display name in the recipient's inbox. Genuinely optional (the mail still
# sends, showing the raw address); Brevo caps it at 70 characters. Kept in sync
# with the `business.name` AppSetting by hand — it is on the envelope, which the
# AppSetting table cannot reach at send time without becoming a second source of
# truth for the sender identity.
EMAIL_FROM_NAME="Kumar Enterprises"

# ── Misc ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated origins allowed to call the API from a browser. Empty is fine
# for Phase 1 (the RN app is not subject to CORS); fill this in Phase 2 when the
# website lands.
ALLOWED_WEB_ORIGINS=""
